## Solution

We evaluate
$$L = \lim_{n\to\infty}\int_{[0,\infty)} \frac{n\sin(x/n)}{x(1+x^2)}\,dm(x).$$

**Step 1: Pointwise limit of the integrand.**

For each fixed $x \ge 0$, set $t = x/n \to 0$. Using $\sin t \sim t$ as $t\to 0$,
$$n\sin(x/n) = x\cdot\frac{\sin(x/n)}{x/n}\;\longrightarrow\;x\cdot 1 = x.$$
Hence, for $x>0$,
$$f_n(x) := \frac{n\sin(x/n)}{x(1+x^2)}\;\longrightarrow\;\frac{x}{x(1+x^2)}=\frac{1}{1+x^2}=:f(x).$$
At $x=0$ the expression is understood by continuous extension: $\sin(x/n)/x\to 1/n$, so $f_n(0)=\frac{1}{1+0}=1=f(0)$. Thus $f_n\to f$ pointwise on $[0,\infty)$, where $f(x)=\frac{1}{1+x^2}$.

**Step 2: An integrable dominating function.**

Using the elementary bound $|\sin u|\le |u|$ for all $u\in\mathbb{R}$,
$$|n\sin(x/n)|\le n\cdot\frac{x}{n}=x \qquad(x\ge 0).$$
Therefore, for $x>0$,
$$|f_n(x)|=\left|\frac{n\sin(x/n)}{x(1+x^2)}\right|\le\frac{x}{x(1+x^2)}=\frac{1}{1+x^2}=g(x).$$
At $x=0$, $|f_n(0)|=1=g(0)$. So $|f_n|\le g$ everywhere on $[0,\infty)$, with
$$g(x)=\frac{1}{1+x^2}\in L^1\big([0,\infty)\big),\qquad \int_0^\infty \frac{dx}{1+x^2}=\arctan x\Big|_0^\infty=\frac{\pi}{2}<\infty.$$

**Step 3: Apply the Dominated Convergence Theorem.**

Since $f_n\to f$ pointwise and $|f_n|\le g$ with $g\in L^1$, the DCT permits interchange of limit and integral:
$$L=\int_{[0,\infty)}\lim_{n\to\infty}f_n\,dm=\int_0^\infty\frac{dx}{1+x^2}=\arctan x\Big|_0^\infty=\frac{\pi}{2}.$$

**Conclusion.**

$$\boxed{\displaystyle\lim_{n\to\infty}\int_{[0,\infty)}\frac{n\sin(x/n)}{x(1+x^2)}\,dm=\frac{\pi}{2}}.$$

